"xdebug - unified xdebug request interface\n=========================================\n\nUsage\n-----\n  xdebug -h\n  xdebug -help\n      Print this human-readable help and exit with status 0.\n\n  xdebug -\n      Read one JSON request from stdin and print one XOUT text response.\n\n  xdebug request.json\n      Read one JSON request file and print one XOUT text response.\n\n  xdebug --json -\n      Read one JSON request and print the original JSON response.\n\nRequest / output contract\n-------------------------\n  All xdebug capabilities are requested through JSON:\n\n    api_version / request_id / action / target / args / limits\n\n  Default output is token-efficient XOUT structured text. It reduces JSON\n  punctuation and repeated structure; readability is a secondary benefit:\n\n    @xdebug.<action>.v1\n\n    summary:\n      signal         : top.u.ready\n      analysis_complete: true\n\n  The handler base class renders ordinary summary/data blocks. Trace, value,\n  scope and protocol handlers add source windows or domain tables. XOUT is a\n  compact projection and is not parsed back into JSON. This does not make JSON\n  the preferred AI/MCP format. Use --json only for stable-field programming,\n  schema validation, structured persistence, deterministic machine comparison,\n  exact unprojected nested fields, or an explicit user request. The renderer does not hide\n  returned rows: limits and truncation are expressed by the action contract.\n\n  Successful value.at responses use a compact matrix instead:\n\n    @xdebug.value.at.v1\n\n    name         100ns  120ns\n    top.u.valid  1'h1   1'h0\n    top.u.ready  1'h0   missing_value\n\n  The first column is the signal or semantic alias; subsequent columns follow\n  requested time order. Request JSON only when its structured fields are needed.\n\n  Use --json to obtain the JSON object for the structured use cases above.\n\n  Machine-readable help remains available through JSON actions:\n\n    {\"api_version\":\"xdebug.v1\",\"action\":\"actions\"}\n    {\"api_version\":\"xdebug.v1\",\"action\":\"schema\",\"args\":{\"action\":\"signal.statistics\",\"kind\":\"request\"}}\n\nShell setup\n-----------\n  Use <xverif-root> for your local repository root. Do not hard-code a\n  personal machine path in shared docs or scripts.\n\n  Bash (~/.bashrc):\n\n    export XVERIF_HOME=<xverif-root>\n    export PATH=\"$XVERIF_HOME/tools:$PATH\"\n\n  Zsh (~/.zshrc):\n\n    export XVERIF_HOME=<xverif-root>\n    export PATH=\"$XVERIF_HOME/tools:$PATH\"\n\n  Tcsh (~/.tcshrc):\n\n    setenv XVERIF_HOME <xverif-root>\n    setenv PATH \"$XVERIF_HOME/tools:$PATH\"\n\nRequest envelope\n----------------\n  Minimal request:\n\n    {\n      \"api_version\": \"xdebug.v1\",\n      \"action\": \"actions\"\n    }\n\n  Typical request:\n\n    {\n      \"api_version\": \"xdebug.v1\",\n      \"action\": \"trace.driver\",\n      \"target\": {\n        \"daidir\": \"simv.daidir\"\n      },\n      \"args\": {\n        \"signal\": \"top.u.ready\"\n      }\n    }\n\nTarget modes\n------------\n  target.daidir\n      Design-side facts. Use for driver/load/query, dependency graph,\n      source evidence, expression normalization, FSM/counter/sequential facts.\n\n  target.fsdb\n      Waveform-side facts. Use for scopes, values, events, APB/AXI,\n      verification windows, signal changes, handshake and anomaly checks.\n\n  target.daidir + target.fsdb\n      Combined/debug join mode. Use waveform time and design causality together,\n      especially trace.active_driver.\n\n  target.session_id\n      Required for all stateful debug queries after session.open.\n      session.close accepts only target.session_id; session selection inside\n      args is rejected by the strict request schema.\n\n  xdebug never silently switches action, surface, transport, backend, or data\n  source after a failure. Obsolete or non-canonical action names return\n  UNKNOWN_ACTION. Choose any different route explicitly in a new request.\n\nSession transport\n-----------------\n  Default transport is UDS. Keep the default for normal same-host debug.\n  Set XDEBUG_TRANSPORT=uds|tcp|file to choose the default transport for newly\n  opened sessions; explicit session.open args.transport takes priority.\n  A transport failure does not trigger automatic switching.\n\n  Use TCP only when UDS sockets are not reachable, for example container or\n  namespace boundaries, inaccessible socket paths, or explicitly requested\n  remote/cross-process daemon access.\n\n  Local TCP session.open args:\n      Set args.transport=tcp with bind_host and port.\n\n    {\n      \"name\": \"wave_tcp\",\n      \"transport\": \"tcp\",\n      \"bind_host\": \"127.0.0.1\",\n      \"port\": 0\n    }\n\n  port:0 or omitted lets the daemon choose an available port. bind_host is the\n  daemon listen address; host is the client-reachable endpoint address for\n  remote/container cases. Do not bind to public addresses unless explicitly\n  needed and authorized.\n\n  Use file transport when the client cannot connect to a compute-node TCP port\n  but both sides can see the same ~/.xdebug session directory:\n\n    {\n      \"name\": \"wave_file\",\n      \"transport\": \"file\"\n    }\n\n  file transport stores a v2 state directory under\n  ~/.xdebug/{design,waveform}/sessions/<session_id>/transport/:\n\n      requests/    client-published pending requests\n      claims/      worker-claimed running requests\n      responses/   unread responses\n      done/        archived request/claim/response history\n      failed/      client_timeout / expired / stale_claim / invalid_request\n      tmp/         atomic write temp files\n      heartbeat/   worker liveness files\n\n  Request, response and heartbeat files are written via tmp file plus atomic\n  publish so readers do not consume half-written files. Old locks/ directories\n  from earlier sessions can be ignored.\n  File request timeout defaults to 300s and can be changed with\n  XDEBUG_FILE_TRANSPORT_TIMEOUT_MS. Ping/quit timeout defaults to 2s and can be\n  changed with XDEBUG_FILE_TRANSPORT_PING_TIMEOUT_MS. History is kept by\n  default; set XDEBUG_FILE_KEEP_HISTORY=0 to discard successful response\n  archives.\n\nAnalysis cache memory\n---------------------\n  XDEBUG_ANALYSIS_CACHE_MAX_BYTES is the soft budget (default 1073741824;\n  0 disables proactive soft LRU). XDEBUG_ANALYSIS_CACHE_HARD_MAX_BYTES is the\n  mandatory hard limit (default 2147483648; must be positive and >= soft).\n  Both values are parsed strictly once when the engine starts. Empty strings,\n  signs, whitespace/trailing characters, overflow, hard=0, or soft>hard make\n  session startup fail; xdebug does not silently substitute defaults.\n\nOutput control\n--------------\n  Responses are compact by default. Use action-specific args.output.verbose,\n  line_limit, or export actions when details are needed. Trace XOUT source windows use\n  XDEBUG_TRACE_SOURCE_CONTEXT_LINES (default 3) and merge same-file nearby\n  source hits with XDEBUG_TRACE_SOURCE_MERGE_THRESHOLD_LINES (default 10).\n  Time-rendering actions use args.render_time_unit=ns|ps|us|auto; the default\n  is ns. This parameter changes only canonical response string rendering, not\n  parsing, sampling, filtering, ordering, ranges, or active-trace decisions.\n  It is available only when the action-specific request schema declares it;\n  no synonymous public field is accepted.\n\nCommon actions\n--------------\n  Catalog:\n      actions, schema\n\n  Session:\n      session.open, session.list, session.doctor,\n      session.kill, session.close, session.gc\n\n  Design:\n      trace.driver, trace.load, expr.normalize\n\n  Waveform:\n      scope.list, nwave.rc.generate, value.at, list.load,\n      event.find, event.export, verify.conditions, expr.eval_at,\n      window.verify, signal.changes, signal.statistics, counter.statistics,\n      signal.stability, protocol.handshake.inspect, signal.anomaly.inspect\n\n  Protocol:\n      apb.query, axi.query, axi.analysis\n\n  Combined:\n      trace.active_driver\n\nAction choice guidance\n----------------------\n  active/high cycles:\n      Use signal.statistics. With args.clock it returns clock-sampled\n      high_cycles/low_cycles/activity; without args.clock it returns raw\n      value-change statistics and sampling_mode explains that behavior.\n\n  counter min/max/average:\n      Use counter.statistics with clock, time_range, vld and cnt. cnt may be a\n      single signal or a restricted {hi,lo} concat up to 64 bits.\n\n  transition timeline:\n      Use signal.changes with mode:\"timeline\" (the default). line_limit bounds\n      data.changes rows. Use mode:\"summary\" for aggregate facts only; summary\n      mode rejects line_limit.\n\n  window holds 0/1:\n      Use window.verify, or signal.statistics for sampled activity. Do not use\n      signal.changes row counts as cycle counts.\n\n  first/last occurrence:\n      Use event.find, or signal.changes with mode:\"summary\".\n      event.find expressions support !, &&, ||, ==, !=, <, <=, > and >=.\n      For valid/ready protocols, legal idle/backpressure is not automatically\n      a stuck bug; prefer valid-qualified event.find, signal.changes and\n      window.verify before using signal.anomaly.inspect as a smoke scan.\n\nOutput control\n--------------\n  Default:\n      Compact summary/evidence.\n\n  Detailed output:\n      args.output.verbose=true only when the action schema allows it.\n      Use action-specific line_limit for bounded rows and export actions for\n      complete datasets. Top-level output, output.verbosity, and public\n      include_* switches are rejected.\n\nExamples\n--------\n  List machine-readable actions:\n\n    printf '%s\\n' '{\"api_version\":\"xdebug.v1\",\"action\":\"actions\"}' | xdebug -\n\n  Query a design driver:\n\n    printf '%s\\n' '{\n      \"api_version\":\"xdebug.v1\",\n      \"action\":\"trace.driver\",\n      \"target\":{\"daidir\":\"simv.daidir\"},\n      \"args\":{\"signal\":\"top.u.ready\"}\n    }' | xdebug -\n\n  Open a session, then query it explicitly:\n\n    printf '%s\\n' '{\n      \"api_version\":\"xdebug.v1\",\n      \"action\":\"session.open\",\n      \"target\":{\"daidir\":\"simv.daidir\",\"fsdb\":\"waves.fsdb\"},\n      \"args\":{\"name\":\"case_a\"}\n    }' | xdebug -\n\n    printf '%s\\n' '{\n      \"api_version\":\"xdebug.v1\",\n      \"action\":\"value.at\",\n      \"target\":{\"session_id\":\"case_a\"},\n      \"args\":{\"signal\":\"top.clk\",\"time\":\"10ns\"}\n    }' | xdebug -\n\n  Join waveform time to active design driver:\n\n    printf '%s\\n' '{\n      \"api_version\":\"xdebug.v1\",\n      \"action\":\"trace.active_driver\",\n      \"target\":{\"session_id\":\"case_a\"},\n      \"args\":{\"signal\":\"top.u.ready\",\"requested_time\":\"100ns\"}\n    }' | xdebug -\n\nLogs and troubleshooting\n------------------------\n  xdebug logs are silent and best-effort. They do not print to stdout/stderr\n  and do not change JSON responses.\n\n  Public action logs:\n      ~/.xdebug/sessions/<session_prefix>_<hash>/logs/actions.ndjson\n      ~/.xdebug/sessions/<session_prefix>_<hash>/logs/stdio.ndjson\n\n  Engine logs:\n      ~/.xdebug/engine/sessions/<hashed-session>/logs/lifecycle.ndjson\n      ~/.xdebug/engine/sessions/<hashed-session>/logs/transport.ndjson\n      ~/.xdebug/engine/sessions/<hashed-session>/logs/crash_marker.ndjson\n      log_health.ndjson in each logs/ directory\n\n  MCP direct/LSF logs:\n      ~/.xverif/mcp/sessions/<alias>/session.ndjson\n      ~/.xverif/mcp/sessions/<alias>/stdio.ndjson\n      ~/.xverif/mcp/sessions/<alias>/lsf.ndjson\n\n  SDK-free loop wrapper logs:\n      ~/.xverif/loop-wrapper/logs/uds.ndjson\n      ~/.xverif/loop-wrapper/sessions/<alias>/session.ndjson\n      ~/.xverif/loop-wrapper/sessions/<alias>/stdio.ndjson\n      ~/.xverif/loop-wrapper/sessions/<alias>/lsf.ndjson\n\n  Helpers:\n      xdebug log doctor --session <id> --json\n      xdebug log tail --session <id> --lines 40\n      xdebug log bundle --session <id> --out debug_bundle.tgz\n      xdebug log bundle --session <id> --out debug_bundle.redacted.tgz --redact\n\n  Troubleshooting order:\n      1. Check public actions.ndjson for action, routing, elapsed_ms and error.\n      2. Check stdio.ndjson for ready, invalid JSON, validate failure and EOF.\n      3. Check lifecycle.ndjson for startup, NPI init, design load, FSDB open,\n         daemon ready, timeout, refresh and restart events.\n      4. Check transport.ndjson for connect, ping, write, read and timeout.\n      5. Check MCP session/stdio/lsf logs for ready timeout, stdout pollution,\n         job id, stderr_tail and cleanup.\n      6. Check loop-wrapper uds/session/stdio/lsf logs for SDK-free wrapper\n         JSONL request parsing, socket handling, LSF job id and cleanup.\n\nDocs\n----\n  README:\n      xdebug/README.md\n\n  Agent skill:\n      skills/xverif/SKILL.md\n      skills/xverif/references/xdebug/overview.md\n\n  JSON API reference:\n      skills/xverif/references/xdebug/json-api.md\n"
